     1	/**
     2	 * stackService.ts — the ONLY place run configurations talk to the rest of the app.
     3	 *
     4	 * On screen this feature is called "Run" and one saved entry is a "run
     5	 * configuration". In here it is still called a stack, because renaming the
     6	 * functions would mean renaming the storage keys they save under and every
     7	 * user's saved entries would come back empty. See the note at the top of
     8	 * `stackStore.svelte.ts`.
     9	 *
    10	 * Two kinds of outside world, kept apart on purpose:
    11	 *
    12	 * 1. **Reading.** `list_runtime_contexts` tells us which processes are listening
    13	 *    on a port and which folder each was started in. That is the whole of this
    14	 *    lane's backend surface, and it is read at exactly three moments: when the
    15	 *    panel is opened, when the user presses refresh, and when a terminal ends.
    16	 *    NOTHING polls.
    17	 * 2. **Starting and stopping.** Spawning a terminal and killing one belong to the
    18	 *    page — it owns the session rail, the terminal service and the terminal
    19	 *    hosts. So this service does not do either: the page REGISTERS three
    20	 *    handlers here (`registerStackHandlers`), and this service calls them and
    21	 *    keeps the bookkeeping. See `_(stacks)-INTEGRATION.md` for the exact wiring.
    22	 *
    23	 * Every backend call is counted with `countInvoke('<command name>')` right
    24	 * before it, so the dev invoke counter stays honest. A `null` result from a
    25	 * `…FromTauri` wrapper means "not running in the desktop app" — nothing was
    26	 * invoked, and the panel says so rather than showing made-up data.
    27	 */
