     1	/**
     2	 * stackStore.svelte.ts — Svelte 5 runes state for the /next run configurations.
     3	 *
     4	 * A RUN CONFIGURATION is one saved way to start something: `pnpm dev`,
     5	 * `docker compose up`, `dotnet watch`. On screen that is the only word used.
     6	 *
     7	 * WHY THE CODE STILL SAYS "STACK". Every name in this file — the storage keys,
     8	 * the exported functions, the `script` field — was written when the feature was
     9	 * called a stack, and the keys are what a user's saved data is filed under. A
    10	 * rename here would mean their saved configurations came back empty after an
    11	 * update. So the vocabulary changed where a person can see it and stayed where
    12	 * only the code can: `stack` in this module means "run configuration".
    13	 *
    14	 * The store holds
    15	 *
    16	 *  - the stacks you have saved, per project folder;
    17	 *  - which terminal session each one was last started in — the tag that makes a
    18	 *    session in the rail recognisable as a stack rather than an agent;
    19	 *  - the processes that are listening on a port right now, as last read.
    20	 *
    21	 * THREE RULES this module exists to enforce (same shape as `browserStore` and
    22	 * `contextStore`):
    23	 *
    24	 * 1. **No backend, ever.** The only IO here is localStorage. Every Tauri call
    25	 *    lives in `stackService.ts` and lands here as a plain mutation.
    26	 * 2. **No `$effect`.** It is illegal in a `.svelte.ts` module and against the
    27	 *    shell rules, so saving is an explicit `persist…()` call at the end of every
    28	 *    mutator that changes something worth keeping.
    29	 * 3. **Nothing loads at launch.** The panel is mounted up front and parked
    30	 *    off-screen; `hydrateStacks()` (storage only) and the service's `activate`
    31	 *    are what wake it up when the user first opens the view.
    32	 *
    33	 * WHY THE SESSION TAG LIVES HERE. `OwnedSession` is shared with the whole shell
    34	 * and this lane may not edit it, so "this session is stack X" is kept in this
    35	 * module, keyed by `ownedId`, and saved under this module's own storage key. If
    36	 * the shell later grows a `kind: 'agent' | 'stack'` field, `stackIdForOwnedId`
    37	 * is the one lookup that has to move.
    38	 *
    39	 * WHY PROCESSES ARE MATCHED BY FOLDER. The backend can only see processes that
    40	 * are LISTENING on a port, and it reports the folder each one was started in —
    41	 * never which terminal spawned it. A stack's shell spawns children with their
    42	 * own pids, so a pid comparison would find nothing. So a port counts as this
    43	 * stack's when it was opened in the stack's folder (or a folder underneath it)
    44	 * AND this stack has a terminal that is still running. Two stacks in the SAME
    45	 * folder will therefore both show that folder's ports; the row says "in this
    46	 * folder", not "definitely yours".
    47	 *
    48	 * Imports are type-only on purpose — the module has none at all — which is what
    49	 * lets `scripts/stackStore.test.mjs` compile and exercise it in plain node.
    50	 */
    51	
    52	// ── Stored shapes ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    53	
    54	/** One environment variable set for a run configuration. */
